            {
              "text": "When choosing a family lawyer in Brisbane for parenting arrangements, child custody disputes, and mediation, it is helpful to distinguish between two roles:\n\n1. **A Family Lawyer to represent you** in negotiations, parenting disputes, or court proceedings.\n2. **An Accredited Family Dispute Resolution Practitioner (FDRP)** acting as an independent, neutral mediator (who can issue a **Section 60I Certificate** under the *Family Law Act* if an agreement cannot be reached).\n\nSeveral of Brisbane’s most respected family lawyers hold dual credentials as **Queensland Law Society (QLS) Accredited Specialists** and **Registered FDRPs/Mediators**:\n\n---\n\n### Top Dual-Accredited Specialists & FDRPs (Mediation & Representation)\n\n* **Deborah Awyzio (DA Family Lawyers / DA Mediation)**  \n  * **Focus:** High-conflict parenting disputes, international/interstate relocation, and complex custody matters.\n  * **Profile:** Consistently ranked in *Doyle’s Guide* as a leading family mediator and parenting lawyer in Queensland. She is a former Vice President of the Queensland Law Society, trained in mediation at Harvard Business School, and conducts private mediations as an independent FDRP as well as legal representation.\n* **Shannon Daykin (Daykin Family Law)**  \n  * **Focus:** Proactive parenting plans, complex parenting negotiations, and collaborative dispute resolution.\n  * **Profile:** A QLS Accredited Family Law Specialist, registered FDRP, and former President of the Family Law Practitioners’ Association of Queensland (FLPA). She is known for pragmatic, child-focused negotiation and mediation.\n* **Judy Stewart (Stewart Family Law)**  \n  * **Focus:** Child arrangements, high-conflict parenting disputes, and child-focused dispute resolution.\n  * **Profile:** Stewart has a professional background in social work in addition to being a QLS Accredited Family Law Specialist and an AMDRAS-accredited advanced mediator and registered FDRP. This makes her particularly suited to matters involving complex family dynamics, child welfare, or communication breakdowns.\n* **Bruce Provan (Page Provan)**  \n  * **Focus:** Parenting disputes, mediation, and arbitration.\n  * **Profile:** A veteran family law practitioner with over 30 years of experience, Provan is a QLS Accredited Specialist, registered FDRP, and nationally accredited arbitrator and mediator, known for guiding contentious disputes to sensible agreements outside of court.\n* **Clarissa Rayward (Brisbane Family Law Centre)**  \n  * **Focus:** Child-inclusive mediation, collaborative practice, and out-of-court settlements.\n  * **Profile:** A prominent advocate for collaborative law in Australia, Rayward focuses on minimizing emotional harm to children and helping separating parents build workable long-term co-parenting relationships through mediation.\n\n---\n\n### Top Brisbane Firms for Complex Parenting Litigation & Negotiation\n\nIf your custody matter involves high conflict, allegations of risk/alienation, or potential court action where you need heavyweight legal representation:\n\n* **Phillips Family Law** (Brisbane CBD) – Consistently ranked Tier 1 in *Doyle’s Guide* for family law. Known for empathetic yet firm management of sensitive parenting arrangements and child welfare disputes.\n* **Barry Nilsson Lawyers** (Brisbane CBD) – One of Queensland's largest and most established family law practices, offering extensive bench strength for contested parenting disputes and appeals.\n* **HopgoodGanim Lawyers** – Preeminent tier practice with broad experience handling complex child custody matters, interstate and international Hague Convention relocation cases.\n\n---\n\n### Key Considerations Before Engaging a Practitioner\n\n1. **The Neutrality Rule:** By law and professional ethics, a practitioner **cannot** act as your independent mediator (FDRP) and then represent you as your lawyer if mediation fails. You must decide whether you are hiring them to facilitate the mediation between both parents, or to advocate specifically for you.\n2. **Section 60I Certificates:** Before a court will hear an application for parenting orders, attendance at Family Dispute Resolution is generally mandatory. Ensure the practitioner you engage for mediation is registered on the **Australian Attorney-General's FDR Register** so they have the authority to issue a Section 60I Certificate if needed.\n3. **Child-Inclusive Mediation (CIM):** If children are of an appropriate age and the conflict is nuanced, ask whether the practitioner offers child-inclusive dispute resolution, where an independent child consultant speaks with the children and presents their developmental needs back to the parents during mediation.",
              "thoughtSignature": ""
            }
